WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.547
Security includes the ability to recover.

2
00:00:02.547 --> 00:00:12.737
A Linux system can be well configured and still suffer from hardware failure, accidental deletion, bad updates, ransomware, operator mistakes, failed deployments, or damaged

3
00:00:12.737 --> 00:00:13.162
configuration.

4
00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:16.134
Backups reduce the impact of those events.

5
00:00:16.134 --> 00:00:23.352
They protect data, configuration, and sometimes whole system states, depending on the recovery needs of the environment.

6
00:00:23.352 --> 00:00:27.598
A backup is only useful if it can be restored.

7
00:00:27.598 --> 00:00:37.787
Restore testing proves that the backup contains the right data, that procedures are understood, that permissions and ownership can be recovered correctly, and that

8
00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:39.486
recovery time is realistic.

9
00:00:39.486 --> 00:00:49.676
Teams should know what is backed up, where it is stored, how long it is retained, who can access it, and what would happen

10
00:00:49.676 --> 00:00:52.223
if the primary system were unavailable.

11
00:00:52.223 --> 00:00:53.921
Change control protects availability.

12
00:00:53.921 --> 00:00:57.318
It is not paperwork for its own sake.

13
00:00:57.318 --> 00:01:06.659
It gives administrators a way to plan risky work, communicate timing, check dependencies, confirm backups, document expected impact, and define rollback steps.

14
00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:13.027
Hardening changes can be valuable, but they can also break applications when assumptions are wrong.

15
00:01:13.027 --> 00:01:17.273
A change window and rollback plan make security work safer.

16
00:01:17.273 --> 00:01:19.396
Exceptions should also be tracked.

17
00:01:19.396 --> 00:01:29.586
A system might miss a patch because an application vendor has not certified it, or a firewall rule might remain open for a temporary

18
00:01:29.586 --> 00:01:30.011
migration.

19
00:01:30.011 --> 00:01:33.832
Those decisions should have owners, expiration dates, and validation.

20
00:01:33.832 --> 00:01:44.022
Recovery readiness ties the course together: least privilege, patching, monitoring, and hardening all matter more when the team can reverse mistakes and restore service.
